---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { currentMonthYear } from '../utils/site-date';
import AppealLetter from '../components/AppealLetter.astro';
import AirportSidebar from '../components/AirportSidebar.astro';

const title = "Appeal Your Heathrow Airport Drop-Off Charge | Free PCN Appeal Letter 2026";
const description = "Received a parking charge notice for dropping off at Heathrow Airport? Use our free appeal letter template and beat your PCN in minutes.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/heathrow-drop-off-charge-appeal/";

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "How to Appeal a Heathrow Airport Drop-Off Charge",
  "description": "Step-by-step guide and free appeal letter template for challenging a Heathrow Airport drop-off PCN.",
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" },
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much is the Heathrow Airport drop-off charge?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "As of 2025, Heathrow charges £5 for up to 10 minutes and £7 for up to 30 minutes in the designated drop-off zone, enforced via ANPR cameras." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I appeal a Heathrow drop-off charge?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. You have 28 days to submit a formal appeal. If rejected, you can escalate free of charge to POPLA, the independent adjudicator." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are valid grounds to appeal a Heathrow PCN?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Valid grounds include: unclear or obstructed signage, ANPR camera error, the vehicle being driven by someone else, a genuine emergency, or that the charge was disproportionate to the duration of stay." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if Heathrow rejects my appeal?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "If your informal appeal is rejected, you can escalate to POPLA completely free of charge. Operators lose a significant proportion of POPLA cases, particularly where signage compliance is questioned." }
    },
  ],
};

const tips = [
  { heading: 'Act within 28 days', body: 'The deadline to appeal is 28 days from the notice date. Miss it and you lose the right to formally appeal.' },
  { heading: 'Use recorded post', body: "If sending by post, always use Royal Mail Tracked or Signed For. Keep your proof of postage." },
  { heading: 'Request the evidence', body: 'Ask for the ANPR photographs in your appeal. Operators must provide them, and they sometimes reveal errors.' },
  { heading: 'POPLA is free', body: "If rejected, you can escalate to POPLA at no cost. Do not pay before exhausting this option." },
  { heading: 'Keep a copy', body: "Save or print a copy of everything you send. You'll need it if the case escalates." },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  currentPage="heathrow"
  schema={[articleSchema, faqSchema]}
>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="breadcrumb">
        <div class="wrap"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/heathrow-drop-off-charge-appeal/">Heathrow</a></div>
      </nav>
      <h1>Appeal Your Heathrow Airport Drop-Off Charge</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Received an unexpected parking charge notice after dropping someone off at Heathrow? You may well have grounds to challenge it, and our free template makes the process straightforward.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="main-content">
        <div class="content-grid">

          <AirportSidebar current="Heathrow Airport" tips={tips} />

          <div class="airport-main-column">
            <AppealLetter airport="Heathrow Airport">
              <p>I appeal as the registered keeper of the vehicle. I am not obliged to identify the driver and decline to do so.</p>
              <p>1. APCOA Parking cannot hold the registered keeper liable for this charge. The drop-off zones at Heathrow Airport are on land under statutory control, including the Heathrow Airport byelaws made under section 63 of the Airports Act 1986. The land is therefore not "relevant land" under paragraph 3(1)(c) of Schedule 4 to the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012 ("POFA"). The POFA keeper liability provisions do not apply, and APCOA has no lawful basis to pursue the registered keeper.</p>
              <p>2. This charge is not a byelaw penalty. It is a contractual parking charge, which can only be claimed from the driver. If the airport wished to enforce against keepers under its byelaws, that would be a matter for the airport. APCOA does not own the airport and has not said it acts under the byelaws. The Single Code of Practice (section 3.1.3, Note 2) says operators must not confuse the enforcement of byelaws with the contractual application of parking charges.</p>
              <p>3. The charge was not brought to the driver's attention before the contract was formed. Under Thornton v Shoe Lane Parking [1971] 2 QB 163, terms that are not brought to the other party's attention before the contract is made do not bind them. APCOA must show that the charge was clearly displayed before the vehicle entered the zone.</p>
              <p>4. The signage does not comply with section 3 of the Private Parking Sector Single Code of Practice. In particular:<br>
                - There was no entrance sign showing that terms apply and whether payment is required (3.1.1 and 3.1.2).<br>
                - The charge was not shown in a large, legible font (3.1.3(j) and 3.1.4).<br>
                - The signs could not be read on approach or in the lighting conditions (3.1.3(b) and 3.1.6).<br>
              Without clear terms shown before the driver committed to entering the zone, no valid contractual offer was made. APCOA is put to strict proof, with dated photographs, of the signs in place at the time.</p>
              <p>The registered keeper cannot be presumed or inferred to have been the driver, nor pursued under any interpretation of agency law. The Notice to Keeper can only hold the driver liable. Any reliance on Elliott v Loake would not assist: that was a criminal case decided on forensic and other evidence, not a presumption that the keeper was driving. APCOA has produced no evidence of who was driving.</p>
              <p>I require this PCN to be cancelled and ask for written confirmation.</p>
            </AppealLetter>

            <div class="instructions-card">
              <h2>How to use this appeal template</h2>
              <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">Click <strong>"Copy template"</strong> above, the full appeal is copied to your clipboard.</div></div>
              <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">Log into the operator's online appeal system (or open your PCN) and paste the template into the appeal text box.</div></div>
              <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">Submit and wait for a decision. The operator must respond within 35 days.</div></div>
              <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">If rejected, request your POPLA code and escalate to the free, independent adjudicator, operators lose the vast majority of cases on these grounds.</div></div>
            </div>

            <div class="airport-info-panel">
            <section class="intro-section">
              <h2>Heathrow drop-off charge appeal</h2>
              <p>Got a PCN for dropping someone off at Heathrow? It's highly likely you can cancel it. Use the free template above.</p>

              <h3>The charge</h3>
              <p>Heathrow charges £7 each time a vehicle enters the terminal drop-off areas. This has been the price since 1 January 2026, up from £6. You must pay by midnight the following day.</p>
              <p>Heathrow uses ANPR cameras in its terminal drop-off zones.</p>
              <p>This is a <strong>private parking charge</strong>. It is not a council fine.</p>

              <h3>How you get a PCN</h3>
              <p>There are two ways:
                <ul>
                  <li>You don't pay by midnight the day after your visit.</li>
                  <li>You stay longer than 10 minutes.</li>
                </ul>
              </p>
              <p>The PCN is £80, or £40 within 14 days.</p>
              <p>Heathrow says it issues PCNs to vehicle owners. That means the keeper. <a href="https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/blog/keeper-liability/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Why that is unenforceable at an airport.</a></p>

              <h3>Who runs it</h3>
              <p>APCOA Parking runs the charge on Heathrow's behalf.</p>

              <h3>Why you can appeal</h3>
              <p>Heathrow's terminal drop-off zones are on roads regulated by traffic orders and airport byelaws. This can affect whether the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012 can be used to hold the keeper liable.</p>
              <p>You do not need to name who was driving, nor do they have any right to ask. This means that they cannot enforce keeper liability, as they can only confirm that you're the owner of the vehicle - <strong>not the driver.</strong></p>
              <p>Other points can also help:</p>

              <ul>
                <li><strong>Unclear signs.</strong> Check whether the charge and its terms were clear before you entered the zone.</li>
                <li><strong>ANPR timing errors.</strong> Ask for the photos if the recorded entry or exit time looks wrong.</li>
                <li><strong>You were not in the zone.</strong> Compare the photos with the location shown on Heathrow's website.</li>
                <li><strong>Emergency or exceptional reasons.</strong> Explain what happened and include proof if you have it.</li>
                <li><strong>The charge was already paid.</strong> Include your payment confirmation.</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Blue Badge</h3>
              <p>Heathrow actually offers a 100% discount for registered Blue Badge holders. Heathrow says applications for an account must be made at least 7 days before the visit. The vehicle can't be left unattended.</p>

              <h3>Free alternative</h3>
              <p>Heathrow says free drop-off and pick-up is available in the Park & Ride car parks (formerly Long Stay). There is a free bus to the terminal.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="grounds-section sources-section">
              <h2>Sources</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Heathrow, <strong>Terminal Drop-Off Charge</strong>: <a href="https://www.heathrow.com/transport-and-directions/terminal-drop-off-charge" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.heathrow.com/transport-and-directions/terminal-drop-off-charge</a></li>
                <li>Heathrow, <strong>Terminal Drop-Off Charge Terms and Conditions</strong>: <a href="https://www.heathrow.com/terms-and-conditions/terminal-drop-off-charge-terms-and-conditions" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.heathrow.com/terms-and-conditions/terminal-drop-off-charge-terms-and-conditions</a></li>
                <li>Heathrow, <strong>Byelaws</strong>: <a href="https://www.heathrow.com/company/doing-business-with-heathrow/flight-conditions-of-use/heathrow-byelaws" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.heathrow.com/company/doing-business-with-heathrow/flight-conditions-of-use/heathrow-byelaws</a></li>
                <li>The Single Code of Practice, section 3.4: <a href="https://www.britishparking.co.uk/write/Documents/AOS/NEW%20Redesigned%20Documents/sectorsingleCodeofPractice.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.britishparking.co.uk/write/Documents/AOS/NEW%20Redesigned%20Documents/sectorsingleCodeofPractice.pdf</a></li>
                <li>POPLA, <strong>Appeal guide</strong>: <a href="https://www.popla.co.uk/appeal-guide" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.popla.co.uk/appeal-guide</a></li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            </div>

            <div class="disclaimer">
              <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> AppealAirportPCN.co.uk is a free information resource for UK drivers. Nothing on this site constitutes legal advice. Templates are provided as a starting point, always review your specific circumstances. For complex cases, consider consulting a solicitor or Citizens Advice.
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

</BaseLayout>
